  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 PM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nVis satellite imagery this afternoon is mainly clear as high \npressure encompasses much of the central CONUS in the upper levels \nand surface high pressure remains centered over the Lower Midwest. \nThis has resulted in a pleasant day across Upper Michigan with high \ntemperatures topping off in the mid to upper 40s and dry conditions. \nThis pattern will persist through midweek, resulting in above normal \ntemperatures in southerly flow through the short term period. Daily \nhigh temperatures from Tuesday through Thursday will approach the \nmid to upper 50s. Under clear skies tonight, overnight lows will \nplummet into the low to mid 20s with the exception of upper 20s/low \n30s across the Keweenaw and eastern sector. But, overnight lows \nthrough the remainder of the week will seem balmy with readings in \nthe 30s and 40s. \n\nThe next chance for rain will arrive Wednesday night/Thursday \nas the next low pressure system traverses the Upper Midwest. \nProbabilities have increased a bit for measurable precipitation \nabove 0.10 inches by Thursday night, but chances look better \nlate Friday through the weekend when a deeper trough/associated \nsurface low approach from the southwest. Best chances for any \nsubstantial rainfall amounts will likely be from Friday night \nthrough Saturday when probabilities of precipitation approach 80\npercent. As winds become northwesterly and strong in the wake \nof the aforementioned system Saturday night, expect strong cold \nair advection to support a transition to a rain/snow mix. \nSpecifics this far out become vague, but continue to monitor for\nany changes in snow potential throughout the extended period.",
